<section class="section tools-page">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:980px">

    <div style="padding:1.5rem 1.75rem;background:var(--bg-alt);border-left:3px solid var(--gold-500);border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;margin-bottom:2rem">
      <h2 style="margin:0 0 0.85rem;font-size:1.2rem;border:none;padding:0">Why I list every tool</h2>
      <p style="margin:0;font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.65">I have two reasons. First, you're paying for your website, so you deserve to know what runs it. Second, whether a site brings in calls usually comes down to choices in this toolkit, and the more you understand what's doing the work, the more confident you can be in the result.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Build and code</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Eleventy (11ty) v3</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the static site generator I use to build your site. It combines your content with my templates and produces finished pages that load in a fraction of a second.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Wix, Squarespace, and WordPress build each page when a visitor asks for it, which is slower and has more parts that can break. Eleventy builds every page once, when I publish, so the visitor gets a finished page right away. That speed is the biggest reason my sites score 95&ndash;100 on Google's speed test (PageSpeed) instead of the typical 50&ndash;70.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Vanilla HTML, CSS, and JavaScript</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the core languages of the web, written from scratch for your site. I use no drag-and-drop page builder, no theme marketplace, and no pile of plugins.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> code from AI page builders and template shops can look fine in a demo while carrying 200 KB of unused styles, 12 third-party scripts, and a layout that breaks on an iPad. I write the HTML and CSS so every line does a job. Your site weighs about 200 KB in all, against an industry average of over 2 MB.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">GitHub (private repository)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> where your site's source code lives. Every change is timestamped, signed, and reversible.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> if something breaks in the middle of the night, I can roll back to any earlier version in seconds, and nothing is locked inside a proprietary content system.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Bunny Fonts</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> a font service that respects privacy. It offers the same fonts as Google Fonts, served from Europe, with no cookies, no tracking, and no consent banner.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Google Fonts, the usual default, loads tracking on every page view and brings EU privacy disclosures with it. Bunny Fonts gives you the same typography without that legal exposure.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Hosting, speed, and security</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Cloudflare Pages</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> where your site is hosted. Your files sit on more than 300 servers around the world, and each visitor gets the closest one.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> traditional hosting (GoDaddy, Bluehost, and the like) puts your site on one server in one city, so a visitor 2,000 miles away waits longer for each page. Cloudflare's network makes your site feel local everywhere.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Cloudflare DNS</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the system that connects your domain (yourbusiness.com) to your hosting.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> it runs on Cloudflare's worldwide network, so changes take effect in seconds instead of hours. It also blocks DDoS attacks (floods of fake traffic) before they reach your site.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">SSL / HTTPS (automatic)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the padlock in the browser bar. It means the connection between your visitor and your site is encrypted.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> without it, browsers label your site "Not Secure," and Google ranks you lower. Cloudflare sets up and renews the certificate automatically, so you never see a "certificate expired" warning.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">UptimeRobot</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> an outside service that checks every minute, from three regions, that your site is reachable.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> if your site goes down, I get a text before you notice. My hosting has never had a sustained outage, but if one happens, I'll know at once.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Forms and leads</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Web3Forms</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the service behind your forms. When a visitor sends a contact form, Web3Forms receives it, filters out spam, and emails you the message.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> contact-form plugins are the leading cause of WordPress security breaches. Web3Forms has no plugin to break into, because the form posts straight to its service. You get the email, and there's nothing for me to patch. A hidden "honeypot" field stops bots without making people solve a puzzle (a CAPTCHA).</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Custom intake forms (by trade)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> forms with questions set for your trade. A roofer's form asks about roof type and age, an HVAC form asks about the system's age and the problem, and an inspector's form asks about square footage and timeline.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> a generic "name, email, and message" form means a follow-up call just to gather details. A form built for your trade gives you what you need to quote on the spot. <a href="/sample-forms/" class="inline-link">See eight industry samples</a>.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Search engines and local discovery</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Schema.org structured data</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> labels built into every page that tell Google, in a format it reads, "this is a local business in this city, offering these services, with these hours and this phone number."</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Google's local rankings depend on these details matching exactly across your website, your Google Business Profile, and any directory listings. Many templates leave this out, and I write it for every page.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the official dashboards from Google and Bing that show which searches you appear in, which pages they've indexed, and what errors they found.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> I connect both at launch and submit your sitemap directly, so new pages get indexed in days instead of weeks. I share access with you, so you see the data too.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">XML sitemap and robots.txt</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> two technical files at the root of your site that tell search engines which pages to read and which to skip.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> AI page builders create these unevenly, or not at all. Yours are rebuilt every time I publish a change, so they're always current and always submitted.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Pagefind (on-site search)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the search box on your site. Its index is built when I publish, and searches run entirely in the visitor's browser.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> most site search either calls an outside server (slow, and sometimes a paid service) or queries a database (slow, and only possible on dynamic sites). Pagefind is instant, free, and uses no cookies.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Visitor statistics (no cookies)</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Cloudflare Web Analytics</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> visitor statistics gathered on Cloudflare's servers. It counts visits, shows where they came from, and measures speed.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Google Analytics needs a cookie banner in most places, and most modern browsers block it anyway. Cloudflare's statistics need no banner, aren't blocked, and raise no privacy concerns, and they give you the same numbers.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Umami (included on every site)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> a more detailed open-source statistics dashboard with no cookies, included with every site on the monthly plan. Each client gets a private live dashboard link on launch day.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Cloudflare's statistics cover the big picture (visits, countries, devices, and speed). Umami adds the next layer: top pages, where visitors came from, and events such as "how many people tapped the phone number on the home page?" Both run on every site I build, with no extra fee and no cookie banner.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Maps and service-area pages</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Leaflet and OpenStreetMap</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the interactive map I can add to your service-area page. It shows your shop, the neighborhoods you cover, a radius, or pinned cities.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Google Maps embeds need an API key, charge per page view above a free quota, and load tracking. Leaflet with OpenStreetMap gives you a working interactive map without any of that. <a href="/demo/redcap-roofing/service-area/" class="inline-link">See it live on the Redcap demo</a>.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Service-area pages by city</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> a page for each metro you serve, with local detail about that area, its neighborhoods, and its common problems.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Google penalizes thin "doorway" pages that swap in a city name and little else. A service-area page with useful local content can compete for "near me" searches. <a href="/service-areas/" class="inline-link">See the live examples</a>.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Email on your domain</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Email forwarding (free)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> mail sent to <code>you@yourdomain.com</code> is forwarded to the Gmail, Outlook, or other inbox you already use.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> if you don't need a full mailbox, most domain registrars offer this free. I set it up for you at launch.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Google Workspace or Microsoft 365 (optional)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> a full mailbox at your domain (<code>you@yourdomain.com</code>), plus Drive, Docs, and Calendar (Google) or OneDrive and Office (Microsoft).</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> if you want a full inbox under your business name, either option costs about $7&ndash;$8 per user per month. I set up the email records (MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC) for whichever you choose, and the account belongs to you.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Quality checks and monitoring</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Google Lighthouse</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> Google's free, open-source checker for speed, accessibility, search basics, and good practice, built into Chrome.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> I run Lighthouse before every launch and aim for 95&ndash;100 on every measure. You can run it yourself at any time.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">WAVE (WebAIM)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the standard accessibility checker. It flags weak contrast, missing labels, and screen-reader problems.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> I check every site with WAVE before launch. WCAG 2.2 AA is the legal minimum in many places, and I build it into every site as standard.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Screen-reader testing (VoiceOver, NVDA)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> I move through your site with the same software that blind and low-vision visitors use.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> automated tools catch about 30% of accessibility problems, and a screen-reader test catches the rest.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Business and admin</h2>
    <ul class="tool-list">
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Stripe</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> the card processor for monthly subscriptions and one-time payments.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> it's direct, low-fee, and clear. You get a proper receipt every month instead of a vague invoice from a reseller.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="tool-item">
        <div class="tool-name">Google Drive (your client folder)</div>
        <p class="tool-purpose"><strong>What it is:</strong> a shared folder for your photos, brand files, contracts, and project notes.</p>
        <p class="tool-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> your files are always in one place you can open. If anything happens to me, your photos, brand files, and contracts are right there to hand to another developer.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:1.75rem;background:var(--bg-alt);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius)">
      <h2 style="margin:0 0 0.85rem;font-size:1.25rem;border:none;padding:0">A few things I choose not to use</h2>
      <ul style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0.6rem;font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.6">
        <li><strong>WordPress.</strong> It runs about 40% of the web and has a huge plugin library. It's also the most-attacked platform on the web by a wide margin, it's slow out of the box, it gets fragile as plugins pile up, and it's hard for a non-developer to change one thing safely.</li>
        <li><strong>Wix, Squarespace, GoDaddy site builders, and AI page builders.</strong> They look fine, but technically they're average, and everything you build stays inside their own editor.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Fonts (the default).</strong> It tracks visitors. I use Bunny Fonts instead: the same fonts, with no tracking.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Analytics 4.</strong> It needs a cookie banner, and more and more browsers block it. Cloudflare's statistics give you the same numbers without either problem, though I can add GA4 if you want it.</li>
        <li><strong>Page builders (Elementor, Divi, and others).</strong> They produce slow, bloated code that's hard to maintain. I write the HTML myself.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
